<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Imagawa clan domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1476 CE – 1569 CE (approx.) · capital Sunpu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The domain of the Imagawa of Suruga, a leading Sengoku power whose ruler Yoshimoto was killed by Oda Nobunaga at Okehazama in 1560.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-clan">Imagawa</a>, a branch of the Ashikaga, had long been governors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suruga-province">Suruga</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-yoshitada">Imagawa Yoshitada</a> died in battle in 1476, a succession dispute was settled with help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-soun">Ise Moritoki</a>, later known as Hojo Soun, who secured the domain for the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-ujichika">Ujichika</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-ujichika">Ujichika</a> expanded into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totomi-province">Totomi</a> and issued one of the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku</a> house codes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-clan">Imagawa</a> kana mokuroku of 1526. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-yoshimoto">Yoshimoto</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunpu">Sunpu</a> a cultured capital, took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikawa-province">Mikawa</a>, held the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> as a hostage and allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-clan">Takeda</a> and Later Hojo, becoming the strongest lord on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokaido">Tokaido</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Marching west in 1560, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-yoshimoto">Yoshimoto</a> was killed in a surprise attack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-okehazama">Okehazama</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-ujizane">Ujizane</a> lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunpu">Sunpu</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a> in 1568 and surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kakegawa-castle">Kakegawa</a> castle to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> in 1569, ending the domain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imagawa-clan-domain.jpg" alt="Imagawa clan domain" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Mukai · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_crest_Imagawa_Akadori.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1476 CE – 1569 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sunpu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Imagawa Ujichika, Imagawa Yoshimoto, Imagawa Ujizane</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Imagawa clan, Imagawa of Suruga</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1522&amp;focus=imagawa-clan-domain" aria-label="Imagawa clan domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8164 886 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imagawa clan domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8500 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8484 1142h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8484 1142h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Japan. <a href="/atlas?year=1522&amp;focus=imagawa-clan-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
